where s is drawdown (ft); Q is the pumping rate expressed in cubic feet per day (one gallon per minute equals {{ meta.gpm_to_cfd }} cubic feet per day); T is transmissivity (ft2/day); S is storativity (dimensionless); r is the distance from the pumping well (ft); and t is the elapsed pumping time (days). Drawdown at a pumped well is evaluated at the well's effective radius ({{ scen.r_w_text }}), which represents theoretical aquifer drawdown at the borehole wall and does not include well losses.

The Theis solution rests on the following assumptions (Driscoll, 1986): the aquifer is homogeneous, isotropic, of uniform thickness and infinite areal extent; the potentiometric surface is initially horizontal; the well fully penetrates the aquifer and is pumped at a constant rate; water is released from storage instantaneously with decline in head; flow to the well is laminar; and the aquifer receives no recharge during pumping. The last assumption is conservative for the Gulf Coast Aquifer System: as pumping continues, an increasing share of the withdrawal is supplied by capture of natural discharge and induced recharge rather than by storage depletion, and water levels eventually approach a new equilibrium (Theis, 1940; Konikow and Leake, 2014). The time required to reach that equilibrium depends on the aquifer's dimensions, hydraulic diffusivity and boundary conditions and can range from days to centuries (Bredehoeft and Durbin, 2009; Barlow and Leake, 2012; Walton, 2011). Because all pumped water is assumed to come from storage, the Theis solution tends to overestimate long-term drawdown in an aquifer that receives recharge. The laterally extensive and relatively uniform sands of the {{ target.aquifer_text }} in this area are considered a reasonable setting for an analytical solution of this kind.

{% if meta.mode == 'feasibility' %}Each case was simulated for the fixed planning periods listed in the results tables, with the wells pumping continuously at their anticipated capacity.{% else %}Two pumping durations were simulated for each case, as required by the District: continuous pumping for 24 hours at the maximum allowable rate, and a maximum-production scenario in which the well or system pumps continuously at its maximum allowable rate until the proposed annual permit volume is reached. {{ scen.duration_sentence }}{% endif %} Simulated drawdown is reported at the pumped well(s), at the nearest property boundary, at each District registered and permitted well within the search radius, and as the distance at which drawdown declines to {{ scen.threshold_text }} (the practical outer edge of the cone of depression). Drawdown is reported only for wells completed in the same District aquifer as the pumping well; values listed for wells completed in other aquifers or of unknown completion are shown for completeness but are not applicable, because the hydraulic response in a different aquifer is governed by that aquifer's properties and by the intervening confining beds.
